<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#dirham">Glossary</a> · Term · currency</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dirham</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as dirhams</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The dirham was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> coin of the Islamic world. Large quantities of Abbasid and other dirhams flowed northwards along trade routes controlled by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate</a>, reaching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volga">Volga</a> region, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-europe">Eastern Europe</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a>. The coins reflect the Khazars' role as intermediaries in trade between the Islamic world and northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eurasia">Eurasia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dirham.jpg" alt="Dirham" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: W.carter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Khazar_coin_Spillings_Hoard.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Though independent in all but name, the dynasty never ceased to recognise Abbasid overlordship: it paid an annual tribute of 800,000 <b>dirhams</b> and had the caliph's suzerainty proclaimed in the khutba at Friday prayers.</blockquote><a href="/empire/aghlabid-emirate">Aghlabid emirate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The truce of 1285 cost fortresses and a million <b>dirhams</b> a year, yet the raids went on, and Hromkla was sacked in 1292.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-kingdom-of-cilicia">Armenian Kingdom of Cilicia · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>The economy was based on irrigated agriculture in the river valleys of the south, grain, olives and vines, livestock and textile production, and the emirs struck silver <b>dirhams</b> at Córdoba.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In northern India Mu'izz al-Din issued coins in the local style, including gold coins bearing the image of the goddess Lakshmi and billon coins with a bull and a horseman, while in the west the sultans struck Islamic dinars and <b>dirhams</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/ghurid-sultanate">Ghurid Sultanate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Furs, wax, honey, slaves and silver <b>dirhams</b> passed through Khazar territory, and the state levied a tithe on goods moving along the rivers and caravan roads.</blockquote><a href="/empire/khazar-khaganate">Khazar Khaganate · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…princes came from tribute in furs, honey, wax and captives, which they shipped to Constantinople under trade agreements such as the treaties of 911 and 944, while hoards of Islamic <b>dirhams</b> show the importance of the Volga trade with the Muslim world in the ninth and tenth centuries.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Socioeconomy</a></li></ul></section>
